Dear Dr. Anita Wolf,

I hope you are doing well.

I am writing to ask whether you would be willing to provide a letter supporting my dietary needs related to diabetes.

I live in a licensed room-and-board home where three meals a day are provided. Because meals are included with my housing, I am not eligible for SNAP food benefits. As a result, I depend almost entirely on the meals served at the home and have very limited ability to obtain healthier alternatives on my own.

Many of the foods routinely served are not ideal for managing diabetes. If you believe it is medically appropriate, I would appreciate a letter stating that I would benefit from access to healthier foods, including examples such as:

* Plain oatmeal, shredded wheat, bran cereal, and other low-sugar cereals instead of sugary cereals.
* Brown rice, wild rice, quinoa, barley, and other whole grains instead of white rice.
* Lean grilled chicken breasts, fish fillets, turkey, lean cuts of beef, beans, lentils, and tofu as healthy protein sources.
* Vegetable soup, chicken and vegetable soup, lentil soup, bean soup, and turkey chili instead of instant noodle soups.
* Fresh vegetables, salads, broccoli, spinach, cauliflower, peppers, zucchini, green beans, and other non-starchy vegetables.
* Healthy snacks such as plain popcorn, nuts, string cheese, cottage cheese, plain Greek yogurt, hummus with vegetables, celery with peanut butter, and fresh fruit in appropriate portions, such as berries, apples, pears, and oranges.
* Beverages such as water, sparkling water, unsweetened tea, coffee without sugar, kefir with no added sugar, low-sugar kombucha, Coke Zero, and other zero-sugar beverages.

Your professional recommendation would help document the types of foods that support effective diabetes management.

Thank you very much for your care and consideration.

Sincerely,

Alexander Miller